@@s001 Union-find: disjoint sets
@@s002 ora:operation in progress,dato:root (representative),ok:result
@@s003 Each node is a set of its own. Each set is a tree, and the <b>root</b> is its representative. Here: 5 roots.
@@s004 <code>union(a, b)</code>: I find the roots (a and b), and hang the smaller tree under the other. Both have size 1: I hang b under a.
@@s005 <code>union(c, d)</code>: the same thing. d under c.
@@s006 <code>union(a, c)</code>: roots a and c, equal sizes: c under a. Now {a, b, c, d} has root a.
@@s007 <code>find(d)</code>: I climb d → c → a. With <b>path compression</b> I link d directly to a: the next search costs only one step.
@@s008 Union-find: disjoint sets
@@s009 The union-find (disjoint sets) keeps groups that merge, and answers in almost constant time the question: are x and y in the same group? It is the structure behind Kruskal.
@@s010 The three operations
@@s011 <b>make-set(x)</b>: creates the group <code>{x}</code>, with <code>x</code> as root of itself.
@@s012 <b>find(x)</b>: climb the parents up to the root. The root is the <b>representative</b> of the group.
@@s013 <b>union(x, y)</b>: finds the two roots and links the smaller under the larger.
@@s014 Two elements are in the same group if and only if <code>find</code> gives the same root.
@@s015 make-set, union, find with path compression
@@s016 Why it keeps the trees low
@@s017 Without precautions a tree can become a long chain, and <code>find</code> would cost <code>O(n)</code>. Two rules avoid it:
@@s018 <b>link by size / rank</b>: under the larger root goes the smaller one. The height grows only when two trees of the same height join, so it stays <code>≤ log n</code>.
@@s019 <b>path compression</b>: during a <code>find</code>, each node met is linked directly to the root.
@@s020 The cost
@@s021 With <b>link by size</b> and <b>path compression</b> together, <code>m</code> operations cost <code>O(m · α(m, n))</code> in total, where <code>α</code> is the inverse of Ackermann: it grows so slowly that in practice it is a constant (≤ 4 for any real input). Without path compression but with link by size: <code>O(log n)</code> per operation.
@@s022 The code, side by side
@@s023 This page explains the concept. The Java code that implements it, with a line-by-line explanation and the syntax notes needed, is on the side-by-side page.
